[EventRounds "9"]
[EventCountry "ENG"]

1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 3. dxe5 Nxe5 4. Nf3 Bb4+ $1 5. c3 Bd6 (5... Nxf3+ 6. Qxf3
Bc5 {is eminently playable but the text injects more tension.}) 6. Nxe5 Bxe5 7.
f4 {Overstretching a little.} Bd6 8. Be3 b6 $1 {I was rather proud of this
move.} 9. Bd3 ({Odd points are} 9. e5 Bc5 10. Bxc5 bxc5 11. Qd5 Rb8 {hitting b2
}) (9. Qd5 Rb8 10. Bc4 Qh4+ 11. g3 Qe7 {& ...Bb7}) 9... Qh4+ 10. g3 Qh3 11. Bf1
Qe6 12. Qd5 Qxd5 13. exd5 Nf6 {This position must be verging on won.} 14. Bg2
O-O 15. O-O Ba6 16. Rd1 Rae8 17. Bd4 Be2 {A stupid oversight. I simply
overlooked that White's next two moves were possible.} 18. Re1 Bc4 19. Nd2 Bxd5
20. Bxf6 Bxg2 21. Bxg7 Kxg7 22. Kxg2 {Now Black has nothing.} b5 23. Ne4 Be7
24. Rad1 f5 25. Nd2 Bf6 26. Nf3 Rxe1 27. Nxe1 d6 28. Nc2 Kg6 29. Rd5 a6 30. Nb4
Ra8 31. Nc6 Re8 32. Kf2 Re4 $2 33. a3 $1 ({Black was rather ridiculously
hoping for} 33. Nd4 $2 {leading to a lost king and pawn ending.}) 33... h5 $4
34. Nb4 $1 {Just winning a pawn.} Be7 35. Nxa6 c6 36. Rd3 Kf7 37. Nb4 c5 38.
Nd5 Bd8 39. Ne3 {(Sealed - White had just rejected a draw offer - and was off
to the Great Eastern to get help finishing off the England number one - editor
[presumably Richard W O'Brien - n.b. the time limit in this tournament varied
from round to round - this round was probably played at a rate of 36 moves in
2 hrs 15 mins - JS])} Ke6 40. Kf3 {Threatening Nxf5.} Ra4 {Forced.} 41. Rd5 Re4
42. Rd2 Ra4 43. h3 {With the vicious threat of 44 Nxf5 Kxf5 45 Rxd6.} Bf6 44.
Re2 Bd8 45. g4 $2 ({The first signs of hope. I hadn't bothered to analyse this,
dismissing it as giving me a sporting chance. A slow look at the position had
revealed that I'm in absolute zugzwang and consequently} 45. Re1 $1 {wins
outright. If} Bf6 ({If} 45... Bc7 46. Ng2+ Kf6 47. Re8 {& Nh4 with decisive
penetration}) ({If} 45... Ra6 {, Ra7 or Ra8} 46. Ng4+ Kf7 47. Nh6+ Kf6 48. Re8)
(45... Kf6 46. Nd5+ Kf7 {and only now} 47. g4 $1 {is annihilation}) 46. Nc4+)
45... fxg4+ 46. hxg4 hxg4+ 47. Nxg4+ Kd7 48. Re4 d5 {At least the minor piece
ending would offer counter chances against b2.} 49. Re5 Bc7 {At last diverging
from Bellin's analysis. However, it is only a bluff!!} 50. Nf6+ (50. Rxd5+ Ke6
51. Rxc5 Rxf4+ 52. Ke3 Bd6 53. Rg5 Rc4 {or ...Ra4} 54. Kd3 {just wins.}) 50...
Kc6 51. Rf5 $2 ({And this really throws it away.} 51. Re6+ Bd6 52. Kg3 $3 {
wins outright - no defence to Ne8.}) 51... d4 52. Ne4 Bb6 53. Rf6+ Kc7 54. Nd6
b4 $1 {Drawing.} 55. axb4 {(sealed)} (55. Nb5+ Kb7 56. c4 $18 {Stockfish 13 
(2021)}) 55... dxc3 56. bxc3 (56. bxc5 c2 {0-1}) 56... cxb4 57. cxb4 Rxb4 58.
Ne4 Bd4 59. Re6 Rb1 60. Ng5 Kd7 61. f5 Ba1 62. Ne4 {Source: bulletin} 1/2-1/2

