1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 b6 4. a3 Bb7 5. Nc3 d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Qa4+ Nd7 8.
Nxd5 Bxd5 9. Ne5 a6 {A necessary precaution against the threat of e2-e4
followed by Bb5.} 10. Bf4 ({Black does not fear} 10. Nc6 {because of the trick}
Nc5 $1 11. dxc5 Qd7) 10... Bd6 11. Rc1 $2 ({Superficial. White must first play
} 11. e3) 11... b5 12. Qc2 Qf6 $1 13. Nxd7 Qxf4 14. e3 Qh4 15. Nc5 ({A crucial
moment. I feel that} 15. Ne5 {is necessary, but Black replies} c5 $1 16. Nf3
Bxf3 {and now if} 17. gxf3 ({or} 17. dxc5 Be4 $1) 17... cxd4 18. Qc6+ Ke7)
15... O-O {A curious position. On the queenside Black is strategically lost,
while on the kingside White cannot move. It is not however clear whether Black
can open any lines before White develops.} 16. Rd1 f5 17. Rd2 {Very
interesting play a la Petrosian. White intends Ke1-d1-c1-b1 while Black is
stuck for a plan.} Bxc5 $1 {After long thought. It is a pity to give up such a
bishop but otherwise White can always meet ...f4 with e4.} 18. Qxc5 f4 19. Qc3
c6 $1 {I am proud of this move. The threat is simply ...a5 & ...b4 opening up
a second front. White has no good defence.} 20. Kd1 a5 21. Qc2 $2 {Panicking
in time trouble.} fxe3 22. fxe3 a4 23. Kc1 Bb3 24. g3 {Otherwise 24...Qe1+.}
Qf6 $1 {Source: bulletin} (24... Qf6 {On} 25. Qd3 {to stop ...Qxf1+ comes} Bc4)
0-1

