1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. a3 Ba6 5. Qb3 $5 {A new idea and quite an
interesting one.} c5 ({Probably} 5... d5 {is a more correct response.}) 6. d5
g6 7. Bg5 $5 h6 8. Bh4 (8. Bxf6 {was a sensible alternative.}) 8... Bg7 ({
Certainly not} 8... exd5 $4 9. Qe3+) 9. dxe6 $2 ({This gives White no
advantage at all.} 9. Nc3 {must be better.}) 9... dxe6 10. Nc3 O-O 11. Rd1 Qe7
12. e4 $6 ({And I do not like this move either, creating a gaping hole on d4.} 
12. e3 {was more restrained.}) 12... g5 13. Bg3 Rd8 14. Rxd8+ Qxd8 15. h4 g4
16. Nh2 Nc6 17. Be2 Qd4 $1 ({Now} 17... Nd4 {is natural and strong but Black
has an even better move which wins.}) 18. Qa4 Nxe4 $1 19. Qxc6 (19. Qxa6 Qd2+
20. Kf1 Nxc3 $19) 19... Qd2+ 20. Kf1 Qc1+ 21. Nd1 (21. Bd1 Nxg3+ 22. fxg3 Bxc4+
23. Ke1 Bxc3+ 24. bxc3 Qe3+ $19) 21... Rd8 22. Kg1 ({There is nothing better as
} 22. Qa4 Bxc4 $1 $19) 22... Nxg3 23. fxg3 Bd4+ 24. Kf1 Bxc4 25. Ke1 Bxe2 26.
Kxe2 Qc2+ 27. Ke1 Bf2+ 28. Nxf2 Qc1+ 29. Ke2 Rd2+ 30. Ke3 Rd1+ 31. Ke4 Qc4+ 32.
Ke5 Qd4# {Source: bulletin [I assume the following was the bulletin editor's
comment - JS] Judged by many as unlucky not to have won the best game prize.
Apparently poor moves by White on moves 12 & 15 help sway the judges. The
consolation for the winner is that it left him a point clear of the field with
just one round to go.} 0-1

